= Hash function =

== Overview ==
'''Hash function''' maps input data of arbitrary or variable length to a fixed-size or bounded-size output called a hash value or digest.

General-purpose hash functions are used in data structures, indexing, checksums, and partitioning. Cryptographic hash functions add security requirements such as resistance to finding preimages, second preimages, or collisions under a specified attack model.

Hashes are deterministic: the same input under the same function produces the same output. A hash is not an encryption of the input and generally cannot be 'decrypted' back to the original data.

In blockchain systems, cryptographic hashes are commonly used to link records, commit to data, identify content, and support integrity checks.

== Boundaries ==
* hash function ≠ encryption
* ordinary hash function ≠ cryptographic hash function
* collision resistance ≠ mathematical impossibility of collisions

